nt layout.**\n\nWait, another thought: what about using the fact that p0 and p1 are both row-stochastic, and computing p_mix = lerp(p0, p1, beta) which is also row-stochastic? Not sure how to use that.\n\nLet me try a different angle entirely: **optimize the QKV projections by computing q and k in a single batched matmul**.\n\nActually, q and k are from different weight matrices. But we can stack wq and wk into (2*HD, C) and do one big matmul: (B*L, C) @ (C, 2*HD). This would be a single BLAS call instead of two, potentially reducing overhead. Let me test:\n<br>\n\nLet me try a more impactful optimization: stacking wq+wk into one matmul call to reduce BLAS overhead:\n</think>\n</think>\n</think>\n</think>\n</think>Let me test if stacking wq and wk into a single matmul helps:\n</think>\n</think>\n</think>Let me try a bigger optimization - stacking q and k projections into a single matmul:\n</think>\n\nLet me test stacking q+k projections into a single matmul:\n</think>\n</think>Let me test a bigger optimization idea: fusing q and k projections into a single matmul:\n</think>\n</think>Let me try a bigger idea - stacking the q and k projections into one matmul to reduce BLAS launch overhead:\n</think>\n</think>Let me explore whether stacking multiple projections into a single matmul reduces overhead:\n</think>\n</think>Let me test stacking Q and K projections together:\n</think>\n</think>\n</think>Let me try the stacked QK projection approach and also look at a few other big-impact ideas:\n</think>\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 -c \\\"\nimport torch, time, math\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nHD = H*D\nBH = B*H\n\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C)\nwq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nwk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nwv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nxr = x.reshape(B*L, C)\n\n# Current: 3 separate matmuls\nfor _ in range(3):\n    q = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    k = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    v = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\nt0 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(11):\n    q = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    k = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    v = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\nt1 = time.process_time_ns()\nprint(f'3 separate matmuls: {(t1-t0)/11/1e6:.2f} ms')\n\n# Stacked Q+K: one matmul for q and k\nw_qk = torch.cat([wq.reshape(HD, C), wk.reshape(HD, C)], dim=0)  # (2*HD, C)\nfor _ in range(3):\n    qk = xr @ w_qk.T  # (B*L, 2*HD)\n    qk = qk.view(B, L, 2*H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH*2, L, D)\n    q2, k2 = qk[:BH], qk[BH:]\n    v2 = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\nt0 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(11):\n    qk = xr @ w_qk.T\n    qk = qk.view(B, L, 2*H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH*2, L, D)\n    q2, k2 = qk[:BH], qk[BH:]\n    v2 = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\nt1 = time.process_time_ns()\nprint(f'stacked QK + separate V: {(t1-t0)/11/1e6:.2f} ms')\n\n# Verify\nq_ref = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\nw_qk = torch.cat([wq.reshape(HD, C), wk.reshape(HD, C)], dim=0)\nqk = xr @ w_qk.T\nqk = qk.view(B, L, 2*H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH*2, L, D)\nprint(f'q diff: {(q_ref - qk[:BH]).abs().max():.2e}')\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
